# packagebudget judges the 60 s budget (docs/testing.md R1) on a go test
# -race leg's output ($1): only the ok lines carry judged durations, a
# package whose tests failed being already red without it. Cached packages
# print no seconds and are not judged. The allow-list goes by file, as the
# reads table does: awk takes no newline in -v.
# A package over budget that the allow-list does not name is run again by
# itself, and judged by the re-run: over 60 s alone fails on a quiet
# machine, naming both times, and passes with both times and the load on a
# loaded one (loadedmachine), unless YARDR_R1_STRICT is set by the quiet
# run. Then the loaded pass is off: over 60 s alone fails however loaded
# the machine, and on a loaded machine the failure line names the load
# beside both times. The re-run is the leg's
# own command ($2 on: the same flags), with -count=1 so it is timed and
# not cached, and the one package; several are re-run one after another,
# sorted, not in parallel. It shares the leg's hold on the race lock
# (YARDR_RACE_HELD, which the leg set): it waits on nothing again. A
# re-run that fails, or prints no seconds, fails the run: the leg saw an
# over-budget package the re-run did not clear.
